# TuningFork in a notebook

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tuning_fork, "~> 0.1"},
  {:tuning_fork_samples, "~> 0.1"},
  {:tuning_fork_kino, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## Compose

A step sequencer you click. Each row is a track — a drum, a synth note or a recording — and
each cell a step; **Play** loops it, and the cell writes the same music out as
`TuningFork` source underneath, so a grid becomes code you can keep editing by hand.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoTuningFork.ComposerCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir

```

## Live patterns, the Strudel way

One pattern a row, stacked and played on a loop. Edit a row and press **Evaluate** (or
ctrl+enter): the change lands at the next cycle line. A row behind `--` is switched off;
a row ending in `|> scope()` or `|> pianoroll()` draws itself. A piece pasted from
strudel.cc plays as written — see `strudel.livemd`.

