## Live loops, the Sonic Pi way

Named loops, each on its own length, written as `TuningFork.Part` code or in Sonic Pi's
words. Evaluate a loop and it swaps in when it next comes round; the counters and state a
loop reads (`tick()`, `get/1`) are worked out afresh each round, so a loop can walk.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoTuningFork.LiveLoopsCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir

```

## A MIDI file, heard

Point the cell at a `.mid` file and it becomes a `TuningFork.Score` — tempo map, note
lengths, velocity and pitch bend kept, drums guessed by channel — and plays. The written
source shows the `TuningFork.Midi.read!/1` and `to_score/2` calls it stands for.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"e30","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoTuningFork.MidiCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir

```

## The same things from code

Everything a cell does is a call in `TuningFork`. A stage in the notebook:

```elixir
KinoTuningFork.stage()
```

A pattern started on it, swapped at the cycle line, and stopped:

```elixir
import TuningFork.Pattern.Control

TuningFork.Stage.start_pattern(s("bd*4, hh*8") |> gain(0.8), cps: 0.5)
```

```elixir
TuningFork.Stage.update_pattern(n("0 4 7") |> scale("c:minor") |> s("sawtooth"), at: :cycle)
```

```elixir
TuningFork.Stage.stop_pattern()
```

A score rendered to a WAV the notebook plays without any stage:

```elixir
import TuningFork.Part

part(bpm: 112, synth: TuningFork.Voice.new(shape: :saw, cutoff: 0.2))
|> play(:d2, 1)
|> play(:a2, 1)
|> then(&TuningFork.Score.from_parts([&1], beats: 2))
|> TuningFork.Score.render(44_100)
|> TuningFork.Wav.encode(rate: 44_100)
|> Kino.Audio.new(:wav)
```
